IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ahrc-dates`

-- Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 (Cth), Part IIB -- the questions of the person who complains of unlawful
-- discrimination and then goes to court.
--
-- SEAT: the person aggrieved by alleged unlawful discrimination (the worker, job applicant, tenant, customer or student
-- discriminated against), who lodges a complaint with the Australian Human Rights Commission and, if the President
-- terminates it, may apply to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2). Also
-- that person as a class member of a representative complaint or a person on whose behalf a representative application
-- is made. "Is this unlawful discrimination?", "Can I lodge a complaint?", "By when?", "Can my complaint be ended?",
-- "Must I go to the conference?", "Can I go to court, and by when?", "What can the court order?", "Will I have to pay
-- the other side's costs?". The Commission, the President, the court and the Attorney-General get no seat: their
-- opinions and decisions are asked as facts.
--
-- Every exported question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a
-- DEONTIC or an EVENT. The duties with deadlines the person meets are regulative rules run inside questions: s 46PO(2)
-- (apply to the court within 60 days of the termination notice), s 46PI(2) with s 46PM (give the President information
-- within the period the notice specifies), and the 24 months of s 46PH(1)(b) (lodge within 24 months of the acts, or the
-- President may terminate).
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 3(1) (affected person, class member, complainant, representative application, representative
-- complaint, respondent, unlawful discrimination); Part IIB Division 1 (ss 46P, 46PA, 46PB, 46PC(1), 46PD, 46PE(1)-(3),
-- 46PF(1), (1B), (3)-(5), (7), (8), (10), (11), 46PG, 46PH, 46PI(2), 46PJ(3)-(7), 46PK(4)-(7), 46PKA, 46PM, 46PN);
-- Division 2 (ss 46PO, 46POA, 46POB(1)-(5), 46PP, 46PQ, 46PSA (the costs rule substituted by the Australian Human Rights
-- Commission Amendment (Costs Protection) Act 2024), 46PT, 46PU). Out, with reasons, in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986, Compilation No. 58, compilation date 18 September 2026 (includes
-- amendments up to Act No. 87, 2026; register id C2026C00416), deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A03366.txt. No regulations set a number this encoding needs; penalty unit values
-- (Crimes Act 1914 s 4AA) are not deposited, so penalties are given in penalty units.
--
-- Sourced from the Federal Register of Legislation, Commonwealth of Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the President, the court or the
-- Attorney-General are INPUTS ("if the President is satisfied that ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of
-- 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where
-- the text is limited by its own wording or silent is marked "NOTHING-kind" (or is NOTHING in a MAYBE answer).

§ `Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986, Part IIB: the questions of the person complaining of unlawful discrimination`

§§ `s 3(1) -- Is it unlawful discrimination?`

-- s 3(1): "unlawful discrimination means any acts, omissions or practices that are unlawful under: (aa) Part 4 of the Age
-- Discrimination Act 2004; or (a) Division 1, 2, 2A, 3 or 6 of Part 2 of the Disability Discrimination Act 1992; or (b)
-- Part II or IIA of the Racial Discrimination Act 1975; or (c) Part II of the Sex Discrimination Act 1984; and includes
-- any conduct that is an offence under: (ca) Division 2 of Part 5 of the Age Discrimination Act 2004 (other than section
-- 51 or 52); or (d) Division 4 of Part 2 of the Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (other than section 42)."
-- Whether the act IS unlawful under one of those Acts is borrowed: the person says under which Act and Part they say it
-- is unlawful. A "means" definition: anything else is the text's no.
DECLARE `Where the act is unlawful` IS ONE OF
    `Part 4 of the Age Discrimination Act 2004`
    `Division 1, 2, 2A, 3 or 6 of Part 2 of the Disability Discrimination Act 1992`
    `Part II or IIA of the Racial Discrimination Act 1975`
    `Part II of the Sex Discrimination Act 1984`
    `an offence under Division 2 of Part 5 of the Age Discrimination Act 2004, other than s 51 or s 52`
    `an offence under Division 4 of Part 2 of the Disability Discrimination Act 1992, other than s 42`
    `an offence under s 51 or s 52 of the Age Discrimination Act 2004, or s 42 of the Disability Discrimination Act 1992`
    `another law only (for example a State or Territory discrimination law, or the Fair Work Act 2009)`

DECLARE `Is it unlawful discrimination` IS ONE OF
    `yes: acts, omissions or practices unlawful under that Part are unlawful discrimination (s 3(1))`
    `yes: conduct that is an offence under that Division is included in unlawful discrimination (s 3(1))`
    `no: that offence is expressly left out of unlawful discrimination (s 3(1), "other than": the text's no)`
    `no: unlawful discrimination means only what is unlawful under the four Acts and Parts listed in s 3(1) (a "means" definition: the text's no)`

@export Is what I want to complain about "unlawful discrimination" that the Australian Human Rights Commission can deal with (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 3(1))? It covers acts unlawful under the Age, Disability, Racial and Sex Discrimination Acts, and some offences under the Age and Disability Discrimination Acts.
GIVEN `where` IS A `Where the act is unlawful` @desc The Act and Part under which you say what was done to you is unlawful. Choose "another law only" if it is unlawful only under a State or Territory law or the Fair Work Act
GIVETH AN `Is it unlawful discrimination`
`s 3 -- is it unlawful discrimination` `where` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF     `where` EQUALS `an offence under Division 2 of Part 5 of the Age Discrimination Act 2004, other than s 51 or s 52`
            OR `where` EQUALS `an offence under Division 4 of Part 2 of the Disability Discrimination Act 1992, other than s 42`
        THEN `yes: conduct that is an offence under that Division is included in unlawful discrimination (s 3(1))`
        IF `where` EQUALS `an offence under s 51 or s 52 of the Age Discrimination Act 2004, or s 42 of the Disability Discrimination Act 1992`
        THEN `no: that offence is expressly left out of unlawful discrimination (s 3(1), "other than": the text's no)`
        IF `where` EQUALS `another law only (for example a State or Territory discrimination law, or the Fair Work Act 2009)`
        THEN `no: unlawful discrimination means only what is unlawful under the four Acts and Parts listed in s 3(1) (a "means" definition: the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: acts, omissions or practices unlawful under that Part are unlawful discrimination (s 3(1))`

GIVEN `where` IS A `Where the act is unlawful`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is unlawful discrimination` `where` IF
        `s 3 -- is it unlawful discrimination` `where` EQUALS `yes: acts, omissions or practices unlawful under that Part are unlawful discrimination (s 3(1))`
    OR  `s 3 -- is it unlawful discrimination` `where` EQUALS `yes: conduct that is an offence under that Division is included in unlawful discrimination (s 3(1))`

§§ `ss 46P, 46PB, 46PD -- Can I lodge a complaint?`

-- s 46P(1): "A written complaint may be lodged with the Commission: (a) alleging: (i) that one or more acts have been
-- done; or (ii) that one or more omissions or practices have occurred; and (b) alleging that those acts, omissions or
-- practices are unlawful discrimination." (1A): "It must be reasonably arguable that the alleged acts, omissions or
-- practices are unlawful discrimination." (1B): "The complaint must set out, as fully as practicable, the details of the
-- alleged acts, omissions or practices."
-- s 46P(2): lodged "(a) by a person aggrieved ... (i) on that person's own behalf; or (ii) on behalf of that person and
-- one or more other persons who are also aggrieved ...; or (b) by 2 or more persons aggrieved ...; or (c) by a person or
-- trade union on behalf of one or more other persons aggrieved". (3): "A person who is a class member for a
-- representative complaint is not entitled to lodge a separate complaint in respect of the same subject matter."
-- s 3(1): "representative complaint means a complaint lodged on behalf of at least one person who is not a
-- complainant." "complainant ... means a person who lodged the complaint".
-- s 46PB(1): "A representative complaint may be lodged under section 46P only if: (a) the class members have complaints
-- against the same person; and (b) all the complaints are in respect of, or arise out of, the same, similar or related
-- circumstances; and (c) all the complaints give rise to a substantial common issue of law or fact." (2): it "must: (a)
-- describe or otherwise identify the class members; and (b) specify the nature of the complaints made on behalf of the
-- class members; and (c) specify the nature of the relief sought." (4): it "may be lodged without the consent of class
-- members".
-- s 46PD: "the Commission must refer the complaint to the President."
-- Plain gloss: you, or several of you, or a person or union for you, may put in writing a complaint of acts that are
-- arguably unlawful discrimination, with the details. A complaint for people who are not lodging it themselves is a
-- representative complaint, with extra conditions.
DECLARE `Who lodges the complaint` IS ONE OF
    `I am aggrieved and lodge it for myself only`
    `I am aggrieved and lodge it for myself and other people also aggrieved`
    `I am one of 2 or more aggrieved people lodging it together, for ourselves only`
    `I am one of 2 or more aggrieved people lodging it together, for ourselves and other people also aggrieved`
    `I lodge it, as a person or a trade union, for one or more other people who are aggrieved`
    `I lodge it, but neither I nor anyone it is lodged for is aggrieved`

GIVEN `who` IS A `Who lodges the complaint`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is a representative complaint` `who` IF
        `who` EQUALS `I am aggrieved and lodge it for myself and other people also aggrieved`
    OR  `who` EQUALS `I am one of 2 or more aggrieved people lodging it together, for ourselves and other people also aggrieved`
    OR  `who` EQUALS `I lodge it, as a person or a trade union, for one or more other people who are aggrieved`

DECLARE `May I lodge this complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may lodge it with the Commission, which must refer it to the President (ss 46P, 46PD)`
    `yes: you may lodge it as a representative complaint, without the class members' consent (ss 46P, 46PB(4)); the Commission must refer it to the President (s 46PD)`
    `no: as a class member of a representative complaint you are not entitled to lodge a separate complaint about the same subject matter (s 46P(3), the text's no)`
    `no: it must allege unlawful discrimination, which s 3(1) defines (s 46P(1)(b), a "means" definition: the text's no)`
    `s 46P(2) does not list you: a complaint is lodged by or for a person aggrieved (NOTHING-kind)`
    `not yet: the complaint must be in writing (s 46P(1)); if you need help to put it in writing the Commission must take reasonable steps to help you (s 46P(4)) (NOTHING-kind)`
    `no: it must be reasonably arguable that what you allege is unlawful discrimination (s 46P(1A), the text's no: fork F1)`
    `not as it stands: the complaint must set out the details of what was done as fully as practicable (s 46P(1B), the text's no: fork F1)`
    `no: a representative complaint may be lodged only if the class members complain against the same person, of the same, similar or related circumstances, with a substantial common issue of law or fact (s 46PB(1), "only if": the text's no)`
    `not as it stands: a representative complaint must identify the class members and specify their complaints and the relief sought (s 46PB(2), the text's no: fork F1)`

@export Can I lodge a complaint of unlawful discrimination with the Australian Human Rights Commission (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 ss 46P, 46PB)? A written complaint, reasonably arguable, with the details, may be lodged by a person aggrieved, by several of them, or by a person or trade union for them; a complaint for others is a representative complaint with further conditions.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who lodges the complaint` @desc Who is lodging the complaint and for whom ("aggrieved" means the acts, omissions or practices complained of were done to or affected that person)
      `where` IS A `Where the act is unlawful` @desc The Act and Part under which you say what was done is unlawful
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint is in writing
      `reasonably arguable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is reasonably arguable that what you allege is unlawful discrimination
      `details set out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint sets out the details of what was done, as fully as practicable
      `already a class member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are already a class member of a representative complaint about the same subject matter (and have not withdrawn from it)
      `same respondent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a complaint on behalf of others: whether everyone it is lodged for complains against the same person
      `related circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a complaint on behalf of others: whether all the complaints arise out of the same, similar or related circumstances
      `common issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a complaint on behalf of others: whether all the complaints raise a substantial common issue of law or fact
      `class described` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a complaint on behalf of others: whether it identifies the people it is lodged for (names and numbers are not needed) and specifies their complaints and the relief sought
GIVETH A `May I lodge this complaint`
`ss 46P, 46PB -- may I lodge this complaint` `who` `where` `in writing` `reasonably arguable` `details set out` `already a class member` `same respondent` `related circumstances` `common issue` `class described` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `already a class member`
        THEN `no: as a class member of a representative complaint you are not entitled to lodge a separate complaint about the same subject matter (s 46P(3), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT (`it is unlawful discrimination` `where`))
        THEN `no: it must allege unlawful discrimination, which s 3(1) defines (s 46P(1)(b), a "means" definition: the text's no)`
        IF `who` EQUALS `I lodge it, but neither I nor anyone it is lodged for is aggrieved`
        THEN `s 46P(2) does not list you: a complaint is lodged by or for a person aggrieved (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF (NOT `in writing`)
        THEN `not yet: the complaint must be in writing (s 46P(1)); if you need help to put it in writing the Commission must take reasonable steps to help you (s 46P(4)) (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF (NOT `reasonably arguable`)
        THEN `no: it must be reasonably arguable that what you allege is unlawful discrimination (s 46P(1A), the text's no: fork F1)`
        IF (NOT `details set out`)
        THEN `not as it stands: the complaint must set out the details of what was done as fully as practicable (s 46P(1B), the text's no: fork F1)`
        IF `it is a representative complaint` `who` AND (NOT (`same respondent` AND `related circumstances` AND `common issue`))
        THEN `no: a representative complaint may be lodged only if the class members complain against the same person, of the same, similar or related circumstances, with a substantial common issue of law or fact (s 46PB(1), "only if": the text's no)`
        IF `it is a representative complaint` `who` AND (NOT `class described`)
        THEN `not as it stands: a representative complaint must identify the class members and specify their complaints and the relief sought (s 46PB(2), the text's no: fork F1)`
        IF `it is a representative complaint` `who`
        THEN `yes: you may lodge it as a representative complaint, without the class members' consent (ss 46P, 46PB(4)); the Commission must refer it to the President (s 46PD)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you may lodge it with the Commission, which must refer it to the President (ss 46P, 46PD)`

-- s 46P(4): "If it appears to the Commission that: (a) a person wishes to make a complaint under subsection (1); and (b)
-- the person requires assistance to formulate the complaint or to reduce it to writing; the Commission must take
-- reasonable steps to provide appropriate assistance to the person." An "if": when it fails, the duty does not arise
-- (the text's no). How it appears to the Commission is asked as a fact.
@export Must the Australian Human Rights Commission help me write my complaint (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46P(4))? If it appears to the Commission that you want to complain and need help to formulate it or put it in writing, it must take reasonable steps to help. FALSE is the text's no: the duty does not arise.
GIVEN `wish to complain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that you want to make a complaint of unlawful discrimination
      `need help` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that you need help to formulate the complaint or to put it in writing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 46P(4) -- must the Commission help me write my complaint` `wish to complain` `need help` IF
    `wish to complain` AND `need help`

§§ `ss 46PA, 46PC, 46PE, 46PF(3), (4), 46PG -- Changing, withdrawing from or ending my complaint`

-- s 46PA(1): "Any complainant may at any time amend the complaint, with the leave of the President." (2): it "does not,
-- by implication, limit any other power to amend". s 46PF(3): "With the leave of the President, any complainant or
-- respondent may amend the complaint to add, as a respondent, a person who is alleged to have done the alleged acts,
-- omissions or practices." s 46PF(4): "A complaint cannot be amended after it is terminated by the President under
-- paragraph (1)(b) or section 46PH."
DECLARE `May I amend my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes: with the President's leave you may amend it at any time before it is terminated (s 46PA(1))`
    `yes: with the President's leave you may amend it to add as a respondent a person alleged to have done the acts (s 46PF(3))`
    `no: a complaint cannot be amended after the President terminates it (s 46PF(4), the text's no)`
    `not without the President's leave: ss 46PA and 46PF(3) give the power with leave, and do not limit any other power to amend (s 46PA(2)) (NOTHING-kind)`

@export May I change (amend) my complaint to the Australian Human Rights Commission, including to add another respondent (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 ss 46PA, 46PF(3), (4))? You may amend it with the President's leave, but not after the President has terminated it.
GIVEN `terminated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President has terminated your complaint (without inquiry under s 46PF(1)(b), or under s 46PH)
      `adding a respondent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the change adds as a respondent a person you allege did the acts complained of
      `leave given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President has given leave (permission) for the change
GIVETH A `May I amend my complaint`
`ss 46PA, 46PF -- may I amend my complaint` `terminated` `adding a respondent` `leave given` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `terminated`
        THEN `no: a complaint cannot be amended after the President terminates it (s 46PF(4), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `leave given`)
        THEN `not without the President's leave: ss 46PA and 46PF(3) give the power with leave, and do not limit any other power to amend (s 46PA(2)) (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `adding a respondent`
        THEN `yes: with the President's leave you may amend it to add as a respondent a person alleged to have done the acts (s 46PF(3))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: with the President's leave you may amend it at any time before it is terminated (s 46PA(1))`

-- s 46PG(1): "Any complainant to a complaint may withdraw the complaint, with the leave of the President." (2): "The
-- President must grant leave if the President is satisfied that all the affected persons agree to withdrawal of the
-- complaint. The President cannot grant leave unless the President is satisfied that they all agree." s 3(1): "affected
-- person, in relation to a complaint, means a person on whose behalf the complaint was lodged."
-- What follows: a withdrawn complaint is not a terminated one, so no application to the court under s 46PO(1) follows
-- it, and no interim injunction may be granted after it (s 46PP(3)).
DECLARE `May I withdraw my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the President must give leave to withdraw, as all the affected persons agree (s 46PG(2)); a withdrawn complaint cannot go to court under s 46PO`
    `no: the President cannot give leave to withdraw unless satisfied that all the affected persons agree (s 46PG(2), the text's no)`

@export May I withdraw my complaint to the Australian Human Rights Commission (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PG)? A complainant may withdraw with the President's leave, which the President must give if, and only if, satisfied that everyone the complaint was lodged for agrees.
GIVEN `all agree` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied that every person on whose behalf the complaint was lodged (including you) agrees to withdrawing it
GIVETH A `May I withdraw my complaint`
`s 46PG -- may I withdraw my complaint` `all agree` MEANS
    IF `all agree`
    THEN `yes: the President must give leave to withdraw, as all the affected persons agree (s 46PG(2)); a withdrawn complaint cannot go to court under s 46PO`
    ELSE `no: the President cannot give leave to withdraw unless satisfied that all the affected persons agree (s 46PG(2), the text's no)`

-- s 46PC(1): "A class member may, by notice in writing to the Commission, withdraw from a representative complaint at any
-- time before the President terminates the complaint under paragraph 46PF(1)(b) or section 46PH." A period the text
-- fixes: after termination the answer is the text's no. Without written notice: the grant is limited by its own wording
-- (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `May I leave the representative complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may withdraw from the representative complaint by written notice to the Commission (s 46PC(1)); you may then lodge your own complaint`
    `no: a class member may withdraw only before the President terminates the complaint (s 46PC(1), a period the text fixes: the text's no)`
    `not yet: withdrawal is by notice in writing to the Commission (s 46PC(1)) (NOTHING-kind)`

@export I am a class member of a representative complaint lodged on my behalf: may I withdraw from it (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PC(1))? A class member may withdraw by written notice to the Commission at any time before the President terminates the complaint.
GIVEN `terminated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President has already terminated the representative complaint (without inquiry under s 46PF(1)(b), or under s 46PH)
      `written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you give, or have given, the Commission a notice in writing that you withdraw
GIVETH A `May I leave the representative complaint`
`s 46PC -- may I withdraw from the representative complaint` `terminated` `written notice` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `terminated`
        THEN `no: a class member may withdraw only before the President terminates the complaint (s 46PC(1), a period the text fixes: the text's no)`
        IF `written notice`
        THEN `yes: you may withdraw from the representative complaint by written notice to the Commission (s 46PC(1)); you may then lodge your own complaint`
        OTHERWISE `not yet: withdrawal is by notice in writing to the Commission (s 46PC(1)) (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 46PE(1): "This section applies to a complaint if any of the respondents to the complaint is: (a) the President; or
-- (b) the Commission; or (c) a Commissioner." (2): "If any complainant makes a written request to the President for
-- termination of the complaint, the President must terminate the complaint, if the President is satisfied that all the
-- affected persons agree to the termination." (3): the President must then notify as s 46PH(2), (2A), (3) require. A
-- complaint terminated under s 46PE may go to court under s 46PO(1)(a).
DECLARE `Must my complaint against the Commission be ended` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the President must terminate it and notify you with reasons (s 46PE(2), (3)); you may then apply to the court (s 46PO)`
    `no: s 46PE applies only to a complaint against the President, the Commission or a Commissioner (s 46PE(1), "if": the text's no)`
    `no: s 46PE(2) requires termination only on a complainant's written request, with the President satisfied that all the affected persons agree (s 46PE(2), "if": the text's no)`

@export My complaint is against the Australian Human Rights Commission, its President or a Commissioner: must the President end it if I ask (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PE)? On a complainant's written request, the President must terminate it if satisfied that everyone it was lodged for agrees; you can then go to court.
GIVEN `against the Commission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any respondent to your complaint is the President, the Commission or a Commissioner
      `written request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a complainant has asked the President in writing to terminate the complaint
      `all agree` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied that every person on whose behalf the complaint was lodged agrees to its termination
GIVETH A `Must my complaint against the Commission be ended`
`s 46PE -- must my complaint against the Commission be ended` `against the Commission` `written request` `all agree` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `against the Commission`)
        THEN `no: s 46PE applies only to a complaint against the President, the Commission or a Commissioner (s 46PE(1), "if": the text's no)`
        IF `written request` AND `all agree`
        THEN `yes: the President must terminate it and notify you with reasons (s 46PE(2), (3)); you may then apply to the court (s 46PO)`
        OTHERWISE `no: s 46PE(2) requires termination only on a complainant's written request, with the President satisfied that all the affected persons agree (s 46PE(2), "if": the text's no)`

§§ `s 46PF -- What the President does with my complaint`

-- s 46PF(1): the President must "(a) consider whether to inquire into the complaint, having regard to the matters
-- referred to in section 46PH; and (b) if the President is of the opinion that the complaint should be terminated --
-- terminate the complaint without inquiry; and (c) unless the President terminates the complaint under paragraph (b) or
-- section 46PH -- inquire into the complaint and attempt to conciliate the complaint." (1B): on termination under (1)(b)
-- the President must notify as s 46PH(2), (2A), (3) require. (5): "The President may decide not to inquire into the
-- complaint, or ... not to continue to inquire ..., if: (a) the President is satisfied that the person aggrieved ... does
-- not want the President to inquire, or to continue to inquire ...; or (b) the President is satisfied that the complaint
-- has been settled or resolved." (6): the President "must act fairly" to complainant and respondent.
DECLARE `What will the President do with my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `terminated without inquiry: the President must notify you in writing with reasons, and you may apply to the court (ss 46PF(1)(b), (1B), 46PO)`
    `terminated under s 46PH: the President must notify you in writing with reasons, and you may apply to the court (ss 46PH(2), 46PO)`
    `the President may decide not to inquire, or not to continue, as you do not want an inquiry or the complaint is settled or resolved (s 46PF(5))`
    `the President must inquire into your complaint and attempt to conciliate it (s 46PF(1)(c))`

@export What will the President of the Australian Human Rights Commission do with my complaint (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PF)? Unless the President terminates it, the President must inquire into it and try to conciliate it; the President may stop if you do not want an inquiry or it has been settled.
GIVEN `terminate without inquiry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is of the opinion that your complaint should be terminated without inquiry
      `terminated under s 46PH` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President terminates your complaint on a ground in s 46PH (for example lateness, or no reasonable prospect of conciliation)
      `I do not want an inquiry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied that you (the person aggrieved) do not want the President to inquire, or to continue to inquire, into the complaint
      `settled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied that the complaint has been settled or resolved
GIVETH A `What will the President do with my complaint`
`s 46PF -- what will the President do with my complaint` `terminate without inquiry` `terminated under s 46PH` `I do not want an inquiry` `settled` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `terminate without inquiry`
        THEN `terminated without inquiry: the President must notify you in writing with reasons, and you may apply to the court (ss 46PF(1)(b), (1B), 46PO)`
        IF `terminated under s 46PH`
        THEN `terminated under s 46PH: the President must notify you in writing with reasons, and you may apply to the court (ss 46PH(2), 46PO)`
        IF `I do not want an inquiry` OR `settled`
        THEN `the President may decide not to inquire, or not to continue, as you do not want an inquiry or the complaint is settled or resolved (s 46PF(5))`
        OTHERWISE `the President must inquire into your complaint and attempt to conciliate it (s 46PF(1)(c))`

-- s 46PF(10)(b): the President "must use the President's best endeavours to finish dealing with the complaint within 12
-- months after the complaint was referred to the President under section 46PD." (11): "Subsections (6) and (10) do not
-- impose a duty on the President that is enforceable in court." So the date is a target, not a right.
@export By when should the President of the Australian Human Rights Commission try to finish dealing with my complaint (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PF(10)(b), (11))? The President must use best endeavours to finish within 12 months after the complaint was referred to the President; the duty cannot be enforced in court.
GIVEN `referred on` IS A DATE @desc The day your complaint was referred to the President (the Commission must refer every complaint lodged, s 46PD)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 46PF(10) -- the 12-month target for finishing my complaint` `referred on` MEANS `referred on` `plus calendar months` 12

-- s 46PF(7): "If the President has decided to inquire into a complaint, the President: (a) must notify the complaint to
-- the respondent, unless the President is satisfied that notification would be likely to prejudice the safety of a
-- person". (8)(a): "as soon as the President has decided to inquire into the complaint". The duty's "if" unmet: the
-- text's no (that the duty has arisen). The "unless" met: no duty to notify (fork F3).
DECLARE `Will the respondent be told of my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the President must notify the respondent as soon as the President decides to inquire (s 46PF(7)(a), (8)(a))`
    `no: the President need not notify the respondent, being satisfied that notification would be likely to prejudice a person's safety (s 46PF(7)(a), "unless": fork F3)`
    `not yet: the duty to notify the respondent arises only once the President has decided to inquire (s 46PF(7), "if": the text's no)`

@export Will the person I complain about be told of my complaint (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PF(7), (8))? Once the President decides to inquire, the President must notify the respondent at once, unless satisfied that this would be likely to put a person's safety at risk.
GIVEN `decided to inquire` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President has decided to inquire into your complaint
      `safety at risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied that telling the respondent would be likely to prejudice the safety of a person
GIVETH A `Will the respondent be told of my complaint`
`s 46PF(7) -- will the respondent be told of my complaint` `decided to inquire` `safety at risk` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `decided to inquire`)
        THEN `not yet: the duty to notify the respondent arises only once the President has decided to inquire (s 46PF(7), "if": the text's no)`
        IF `safety at risk`
        THEN `no: the President need not notify the respondent, being satisfied that notification would be likely to prejudice a person's safety (s 46PF(7)(a), "unless": fork F3)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the President must notify the respondent as soon as the President decides to inquire (s 46PF(7)(a), (8)(a))`

§§ `ss 46PI, 46PM, 46PN -- Information the President requires of me`

-- s 46PI(2): "The President may serve a written notice on the person, requiring the person to ... within a reasonable
-- period specified in the notice ...: (a) give the President a signed document containing relevant information required
-- by the notice; (b) produce to the President such relevant documents as are specified in the notice."
-- s 46PM(1): "A person must not refuse or fail: (a) to give information; or (b) to produce a document; when so required
-- under section 46PI. Penalty: 10 penalty units." (1A): "Subsection (1) does not apply if the person has a reasonable
-- excuse." (3): "It is a reasonable excuse ... for an individual to refuse or fail to answer a question or produce a
-- document on the ground that the answer or the production of the document might tend to incriminate the individual or
-- to expose the individual to a penalty."
-- As a duty with a deadline: the person served MUST give the information WITHIN the period the notice specifies, LEST
-- the offence. Days are counted from the day the notice is served (day 0).
DECLARE `Person served` IS ONE OF `the person served with the notice`
DECLARE `Information step` IS ONE OF `give the President the information or documents required`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Person served` `Information step`
`s 46PI(2) -- give the information within the period the notice specifies` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the person served with the notice`
    MUST `give the President the information or documents required`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person served with the notice` BECAUSE "information or documents not given within the period the notice specified: an offence, 10 penalty units, unless the person has a reasonable excuse (s 46PM)"

-- Runs the rule over what happened. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH, so
-- FULFILLED means in time and anything else late. Not exported; it takes no DEONTIC from outside.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 46PI(2) -- giving the information on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 46PI(2) -- give the information within the period the notice specifies` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the person served with the notice` `give the President the information or documents required` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Must I give the information` IS ONE OF
    `you complied: you gave it within the period the notice specified (s 46PI(2))`
    `you must give it: the period the notice specifies has not yet ended (s 46PI(2)); refusing or failing is an offence of 10 penalty units unless you have a reasonable excuse (s 46PM)`
    `no offence: you have a reasonable excuse (s 46PM(1A)); refusing because the answer or document might incriminate you or expose you to a penalty is one (s 46PM(3))`
    `an offence: you refused or failed to give it within the period the notice specified, without reasonable excuse (s 46PM(1)); penalty 10 penalty units`
    `no duty under s 46PM: it applies only when the President has required the information by written notice under s 46PI (s 46PM(1), "when": the text's no)`

@export Must I give the information or documents the President of the Australian Human Rights Commission has required of me, and is it an offence if I do not (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 ss 46PI, 46PM)? A written notice may require you to give information or produce documents within a reasonable period it specifies; failing without reasonable excuse is an offence of 10 penalty units, and self-incrimination is a reasonable excuse.
GIVEN `served with a notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President has served you with a written notice requiring you to give information or produce documents
      `days in the notice` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the notice gives you, counted from the day it was served
      `given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the information or produced the documents
      `days since service` IS A NUMBER @desc If you have given them: the number of days after service on which you did; if not: the number of days since service until today
      `reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a reasonable excuse for not giving them (any reasonable excuse, not only self-incrimination)
      `self-incrimination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you refuse because the answer or document might tend to incriminate you or expose you to a penalty
GIVETH A `Must I give the information`
`ss 46PI, 46PM -- must I give the information` `served with a notice` `days in the notice` `given` `days since service` `reasonable excuse` `self-incrimination` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `served with a notice`)
        THEN `no duty under s 46PM: it applies only when the President has required the information by written notice under s 46PI (s 46PM(1), "when": the text's no)`
        IF `given` AND (`s 46PI(2) -- giving the information on this day is in time` `days in the notice` `the day`)
        THEN `you complied: you gave it within the period the notice specified (s 46PI(2))`
        IF (NOT `given`) AND `the day` AT MOST `days in the notice` AND (NOT (`reasonable excuse` OR `self-incrimination`))
        THEN `you must give it: the period the notice specifies has not yet ended (s 46PI(2)); refusing or failing is an offence of 10 penalty units unless you have a reasonable excuse (s 46PM)`
        IF `reasonable excuse` OR `self-incrimination`
        THEN `no offence: you have a reasonable excuse (s 46PM(1A)); refusing because the answer or document might incriminate you or expose you to a penalty is one (s 46PM(3))`
        OTHERWISE `an offence: you refused or failed to give it within the period the notice specified, without reasonable excuse (s 46PM(1)); penalty 10 penalty units`
    WHERE
        `the day` MEANS IF `days since service` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `days since service`

-- s 46PN: "A person must not give information or make a statement to the Commission, to the President or to any other
-- person exercising powers or performing functions under this Act, knowing that the information or statement is false or
-- misleading in a material particular. Penalty: Imprisonment for 6 months." Outside the prohibition's wording the Act is
-- silent: NOTHING.
@export What is the most I could be imprisoned for if I knowingly give the Australian Human Rights Commission or the President false or misleading information (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PN)? Answers 6 months where the information was false or misleading in a material particular and you knew it; nothing otherwise, as s 46PN does not reach the case.
GIVEN `knew false or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you knew the information or statement was false or misleading
      `material` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was false or misleading in a material (significant) particular
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 46PN -- most months of imprisonment for false or misleading information` `knew false or misleading` `material` MEANS
    IF `knew false or misleading` AND `material`
    THEN JUST 6
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `ss 46PJ, 46PK, 46PKA -- The conciliation conference`

-- s 46PJ(2): the President "may (a) invite any or all of the complainants or respondents to attend the conference". (3):
-- "The President may, by written notice given to a person referred to in subsection (2), require the person to attend the
-- conference". (4): "A notice under subsection (3): (a) must specify the place and time of the conference, not being a
-- time that is less than 14 days after the notice is given; and (b) must set out the effect of subsection (5)." (5): "A
-- person commits an offence if: (a) the person has been given a notice under subsection (3) requiring the person to
-- attend a conference; and (b) the person refuses or fails to comply with the requirement. Penalty: 10 penalty units."
-- (6): strict liability. (7): "A person who is required to attend the conference is entitled to be paid, by the
-- Commonwealth, a reasonable sum for the person's expenses of attendance."
DECLARE `Must I attend the conference` IS ONE OF
    `you must attend: failing to comply with the notice is a strict liability offence, 10 penalty units (s 46PJ(5), (6)); you are entitled to a reasonable sum from the Commonwealth for your expenses of attendance (s 46PJ(7))`
    `you attended as required: you are entitled to a reasonable sum from the Commonwealth for your expenses of attendance (s 46PJ(7))`
    `not compulsory: you were invited, not required by written notice; the offence needs a notice under s 46PJ(3) (s 46PJ(5)(a), the text's no)`
    `the notice does not meet s 46PJ(4) (a time less than 14 days after it was given, or no statement of the offence); the Act does not say whether it still binds you (NOTHING-kind, fork F4)`

@export Must I attend the conciliation conference the President of the Australian Human Rights Commission has called, and what if I do not (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PJ)? An invitation is not compulsory; a written notice requiring you to attend, giving at least 14 days, is, and failing to attend is a strict liability offence of 10 penalty units. You are entitled to reasonable expenses.
GIVEN `written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were given a written notice requiring you to attend the conference (not just an invitation)
      `days of notice` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days after the notice was given at which the conference is set
      `states the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the notice sets out that failing to attend is an offence (the effect of s 46PJ(5))
      `attended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you attended (or will attend) as required
GIVETH A `Must I attend the conference`
`s 46PJ -- must I attend the conference` `written notice` `days of notice` `states the offence` `attended` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `written notice`)
        THEN `not compulsory: you were invited, not required by written notice; the offence needs a notice under s 46PJ(3) (s 46PJ(5)(a), the text's no)`
        IF `days of notice` LESS THAN 14 OR (NOT `states the offence`)
        THEN `the notice does not meet s 46PJ(4) (a time less than 14 days after it was given, or no statement of the offence); the Act does not say whether it still binds you (NOTHING-kind, fork F4)`
        IF `attended`
        THEN `you attended as required: you are entitled to a reasonable sum from the Commonwealth for your expenses of attendance (s 46PJ(7))`
        OTHERWISE `you must attend: failing to comply with the notice is a strict liability offence, 10 penalty units (s 46PJ(5), (6)); you are entitled to a reasonable sum from the Commonwealth for your expenses of attendance (s 46PJ(7))`

-- s 46PK(2): "The conference is to be conducted in private." (4): "Unless the person presiding at the conference
-- consents: (a) an individual is not entitled to be represented at the conference by another person". (5): "Despite
-- paragraph (4)(a), an individual who is unable to attend the conference because the individual has a disability is
-- entitled to nominate another person to attend instead on his or her behalf." (6): "If the person presiding at the
-- conference considers that an individual is unable to participate fully in the conference because the individual has
-- a disability, the individual is entitled to nominate another person to assist him or her at the conference." (7):
-- disability as in the Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (borrowed: asked as a fact).
DECLARE `May someone represent me at the conference` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the person presiding has consented to your being represented (s 46PK(4))`
    `yes: you cannot attend because of a disability, so you may nominate another person to attend instead of you (s 46PK(5))`
    `no, but you may nominate another person to assist you at the conference, as the person presiding considers your disability stops you participating fully (s 46PK(6))`
    `no: unless the person presiding consents, you are not entitled to be represented at the conference by another person (s 46PK(4)(a), the text's no)`

@export May a lawyer, advocate or someone else represent me at the conciliation conference of the Australian Human Rights Commission (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PK)? Not unless the person presiding consents; but a person who cannot attend because of a disability may send someone instead, and one who cannot participate fully may bring someone to assist.
GIVEN `presiding person consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person presiding at the conference consents to your being represented
      `cannot attend for disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are unable to attend the conference because you have a disability (as defined in the Disability Discrimination Act 1992)
      `cannot participate fully for disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person presiding considers that you are unable to participate fully in the conference because you have a disability
GIVETH A `May someone represent me at the conference`
`s 46PK -- may someone represent me at the conference` `presiding person consents` `cannot attend for disability` `cannot participate fully for disability` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `presiding person consents`
        THEN `yes: the person presiding has consented to your being represented (s 46PK(4))`
        IF `cannot attend for disability`
        THEN `yes: you cannot attend because of a disability, so you may nominate another person to attend instead of you (s 46PK(5))`
        IF `cannot participate fully for disability`
        THEN `no, but you may nominate another person to assist you at the conference, as the person presiding considers your disability stops you participating fully (s 46PK(6))`
        OTHERWISE `no: unless the person presiding consents, you are not entitled to be represented at the conference by another person (s 46PK(4)(a), the text's no)`

-- s 46PKA(1): "Evidence of anything said or done by a person in the course of the conciliation of a complaint in
-- accordance with section 46PF is not admissible in any proceedings relating to the alleged acts, omissions or
-- practices." (2): "Subsection (1) does not apply for the purposes of the application of section 46PSA." For proceedings
-- about something else the section is silent: NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `Can what was said in conciliation be used` IS ONE OF
    `no: evidence of anything said or done in the conciliation is not admissible in proceedings about the acts complained of (s 46PKA(1), the text's no)`
    `yes, it may be admitted: the bar does not apply when the court applies the costs rule in s 46PSA (s 46PKA(2))`
    `s 46PKA does not deal with proceedings about other matters (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can what was said or done in conciliation at the Australian Human Rights Commission be used as evidence in court (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PKA)? Not in proceedings about the acts you complained of, except when the court decides costs under s 46PSA.
GIVEN `about the same acts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court proceedings relate to the acts, omissions or practices you complained of
      `for costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence is offered for the court's decision on costs under s 46PSA
GIVETH A `Can what was said in conciliation be used`
`s 46PKA -- can what was said in conciliation be used` `about the same acts` `for costs` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `about the same acts`)
        THEN `s 46PKA does not deal with proceedings about other matters (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `for costs`
        THEN `yes, it may be admitted: the bar does not apply when the court applies the costs rule in s 46PSA (s 46PKA(2))`
        OTHERWISE `no: evidence of anything said or done in the conciliation is not admissible in proceedings about the acts complained of (s 46PKA(1), the text's no)`

§§ `s 46PH -- Can my complaint be terminated?`

-- s 46PH(1): "The President may terminate a complaint on any of the following grounds: ... (b) the complaint was lodged
-- more than 24 months after the alleged acts, omissions or practices took place". A ground for a discretion, not a bar.
-- A complaint lodged on the day 24 months after the acts is not "more than 24 months after": that day is the last day
-- (fork F2). For acts on several days, the person gives the last of them (fork F5).
@export What is the last day on which I can lodge my complaint with the Australian Human Rights Commission without the President being able to end it for lateness (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PH(1)(b))? The President may terminate a complaint lodged more than 24 months after the acts complained of.
GIVEN `the acts took place on` IS A DATE @desc The day the acts, omissions or practices you complain of took place (if they went on over a period, the last day)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 46PH(1)(b) -- the last day to lodge without the lateness ground` `the acts took place on` MEANS `the acts took place on` `plus calendar months` 24

DECLARE `Complainant` IS ONE OF `the person complaining`
DECLARE `Complaint step` IS ONE OF `lodge the complaint with the Commission`

-- The 24 months as a rule with a deadline for the complainant: lodge within 24 months, or the President may terminate.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complainant` `Complaint step`
`s 46PH(1)(b) -- lodge within 24 months of the acts` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the person complaining`
    MUST `lodge the complaint with the Commission`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person complaining` BECAUSE "complaint lodged more than 24 months after the acts: the President may terminate it (s 46PH(1)(b))"

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 46PH(1)(b) -- lodging on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 46PH(1)(b) -- lodge within 24 months of the acts` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the person complaining` `lodge the complaint with the Commission` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the acts took place on` IS A DATE, `lodged on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `lodged more than 24 months after the acts` `the acts took place on` `lodged on` IF
    (NOT (`s 46PH(1)(b) -- lodging on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day lodged`))
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the number of days from` `the acts took place on` `until` (`s 46PH(1)(b) -- the last day to lodge without the lateness ground` `the acts took place on`)
        `the raw day` MEANS `the number of days from` `the acts took place on` `until` `lodged on`
        -- A complaint dated before the acts is counted as lodged on day 0.
        `the day lodged` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

-- s 46PH(1B): "The President must terminate a complaint if the President is satisfied that: (a) the complaint is trivial,
-- vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance; or (b) there is no reasonable prospect of the matter being settled by
-- conciliation." (1C): "The President must terminate a complaint if the President is satisfied that there would be no
-- reasonable prospect that the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) would be
-- satisfied that the alleged acts, omissions or practices are unlawful discrimination."
-- s 46PH(1): the President "may terminate" on grounds (a) not unlawful discrimination; (b) more than 24 months; (c) an
-- inquiry not warranted; (d) another remedy sought and the subject matter adequately dealt with; (e) a more appropriate
-- remedy reasonably available to each affected person; (f) already dealt with by the Commission or another statutory
-- authority, adequately; (g) more effectively or conveniently dealt with by another statutory authority; (h) an issue of
-- public importance for the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court. (1A), (1D): at any time, even after an
-- inquiry has begun. The grounds are reported in the text's order, the mandatory ones first; the leave question (s
-- 46PO(3A)) turns on the ground used.
-- The list in (1) is not closed with "only", and s 46PF(1)(b) lets the President terminate without inquiry on the
-- President's opinion: with no ground found, the answer is NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `Can my complaint be terminated` IS ONE OF
    `the President must terminate it: trivial, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance (s 46PH(1B)(a))`
    `the President must terminate it: no reasonable prospect of settlement by conciliation (s 46PH(1B)(b)); you will not need the court's leave to apply (s 46PO(3A)(c))`
    `the President must terminate it: no reasonable prospect that the court would find unlawful discrimination (s 46PH(1C))`
    `the President may terminate it: not unlawful discrimination (s 46PH(1)(a))`
    `the President may terminate it: lodged more than 24 months after the acts (s 46PH(1)(b))`
    `the President may terminate it: an inquiry is not warranted (s 46PH(1)(c))`
    `the President may terminate it: another remedy sought has adequately dealt with it (s 46PH(1)(d))`
    `the President may terminate it: a more appropriate remedy is reasonably available to each affected person (s 46PH(1)(e))`
    `the President may terminate it: already adequately dealt with by the Commission or another statutory authority (s 46PH(1)(f))`
    `the President may terminate it: another statutory authority could deal with it more effectively or conveniently (s 46PH(1)(g))`
    `the President may terminate it: an issue of public importance for the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court (s 46PH(1)(h)); you will not need the court's leave to apply (s 46PO(3A)(b))`
    `no ground in s 46PH is made out on these facts; the President may still terminate without inquiry if of the opinion it should be terminated (s 46PF(1)(b)) (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can the President of the Australian Human Rights Commission end (terminate) my complaint, and on what ground (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PH)? The President must end a complaint that is trivial or lacking in substance, has no reasonable prospect of conciliation, or would have no reasonable prospect in court; and may end one on eight further grounds, including lodging more than 24 months after the acts.
GIVEN `trivial or lacking substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied the complaint is trivial, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance
      `no prospect of conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied there is no reasonable prospect of the matter being settled by conciliation
      `no prospect in court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied there would be no reasonable prospect that the court would find the acts to be unlawful discrimination
      `not unlawful discrimination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied the acts are not unlawful discrimination (for example because an exemption applies)
      `the acts took place on` IS A DATE @desc The day the acts complained of took place (if over a period, the last day)
      `lodged on` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged the complaint
      `inquiry not warranted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied, having regard to all the circumstances, that an inquiry (or continuing one) is not warranted
      `other remedy dealt with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you sought some other remedy for the same subject matter and the President is satisfied it has been adequately dealt with
      `better remedy available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied some other more appropriate remedy is reasonably available to each person the complaint was lodged for
      `already dealt with` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the subject matter has already been dealt with by the Commission or another statutory authority and the President is satisfied adequately
      `another authority better` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied another statutory authority could deal with it more effectively or conveniently
      `public importance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied it involves an issue of public importance that should be considered by the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2)
GIVETH A `Can my complaint be terminated`
`s 46PH -- can my complaint be terminated` `trivial or lacking substance` `no prospect of conciliation` `no prospect in court` `not unlawful discrimination` `the acts took place on` `lodged on` `inquiry not warranted` `other remedy dealt with it` `better remedy available` `already dealt with` `another authority better` `public importance` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `trivial or lacking substance`
        THEN `the President must terminate it: trivial, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance (s 46PH(1B)(a))`
        IF `no prospect of conciliation`
        THEN `the President must terminate it: no reasonable prospect of settlement by conciliation (s 46PH(1B)(b)); you will not need the court's leave to apply (s 46PO(3A)(c))`
        IF `no prospect in court`
        THEN `the President must terminate it: no reasonable prospect that the court would find unlawful discrimination (s 46PH(1C))`
        IF `not unlawful discrimination`
        THEN `the President may terminate it: not unlawful discrimination (s 46PH(1)(a))`
        IF `lodged more than 24 months after the acts` `the acts took place on` `lodged on`
        THEN `the President may terminate it: lodged more than 24 months after the acts (s 46PH(1)(b))`
        IF `inquiry not warranted`
        THEN `the President may terminate it: an inquiry is not warranted (s 46PH(1)(c))`
        IF `other remedy dealt with it`
        THEN `the President may terminate it: another remedy sought has adequately dealt with it (s 46PH(1)(d))`
        IF `better remedy available`
        THEN `the President may terminate it: a more appropriate remedy is reasonably available to each affected person (s 46PH(1)(e))`
        IF `already dealt with`
        THEN `the President may terminate it: already adequately dealt with by the Commission or another statutory authority (s 46PH(1)(f))`
        IF `another authority better`
        THEN `the President may terminate it: another statutory authority could deal with it more effectively or conveniently (s 46PH(1)(g))`
        IF `public importance`
        THEN `the President may terminate it: an issue of public importance for the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court (s 46PH(1)(h)); you will not need the court's leave to apply (s 46PO(3A)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `no ground in s 46PH is made out on these facts; the President may still terminate without inquiry if of the opinion it should be terminated (s 46PF(1)(b)) (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 46PH(2): "If the President terminates a complaint, the President must notify the complainants in writing of the
-- termination and of the reasons for the termination." (2A): the notice "must include a statement explaining that the
-- Federal Court and the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) can award costs in proceedings under
-- section 46PO in certain circumstances." (3): "On request by an affected person who is not a complainant, the President
-- must give the affected person a copy of the notice". (4): "The President may revoke the termination of a complaint, but
-- not after an application is made to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2)
-- under section 46PO in relation to the complaint."
DECLARE `Must I be sent the termination notice` IS ONE OF
    `yes: as a complainant you must be notified in writing of the termination and the reasons, with a statement that the court can award costs in certain circumstances (s 46PH(2), (2A))`
    `yes: as an affected person who is not a complainant, you must be given a copy of that notice because you asked for it (s 46PH(3))`
    `no: an affected person who is not a complainant is given a copy only on request (s 46PH(3), "on request": the text's no)`

@export My complaint to the Australian Human Rights Commission has been terminated: am I entitled to the written notice of termination (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PH(2), (2A), (3))? Complainants must be notified in writing, with reasons and a statement about costs in court; a person the complaint was lodged for who is not a complainant gets a copy on request.
GIVEN `I am a complainant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you lodged the complaint (alone or with others), rather than having it lodged on your behalf
      `I asked for a copy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was lodged on your behalf: whether you have asked the President for a copy of the termination notice
GIVETH A `Must I be sent the termination notice`
`s 46PH(2), (3) -- must I be sent the termination notice` `I am a complainant` `I asked for a copy` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `I am a complainant`
        THEN `yes: as a complainant you must be notified in writing of the termination and the reasons, with a statement that the court can award costs in certain circumstances (s 46PH(2), (2A))`
        IF `I asked for a copy`
        THEN `yes: as an affected person who is not a complainant, you must be given a copy of that notice because you asked for it (s 46PH(3))`
        OTHERWISE `no: an affected person who is not a complainant is given a copy only on request (s 46PH(3), "on request": the text's no)`

@export Can the President of the Australian Human Rights Commission revoke the termination of my complaint (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PH(4))? The President may revoke it, but not after an application about the complaint has been made to the court under s 46PO. FALSE is the text's no.
GIVEN `applied to court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an application about the complaint has been made to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) under s 46PO
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 46PH(4) -- may the President revoke the termination` `applied to court` IF
    (NOT `applied to court`)

§§ `ss 46PO, 46POA, 46POB -- Can I go to court, and by when?`

-- s 46PO(1): "If: (a) a complaint has been terminated by the President under section 46PE, paragraph 46PF(1)(b) or
-- section 46PH; and (b) the President has given a notice to any person under subsection 46PH(2) in relation to the
-- termination; an application may be made to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia
-- (Division 2), alleging unlawful discrimination by one or more of the respondents to the terminated complaint."
-- (2A): by "(a) an affected person ...; (b) 2 or more affected persons ...; (c) a person or trade union who lodged the
-- terminated complaint, on behalf of one or more affected persons". (3): the discrimination alleged "(a) must be the
-- same as (or the same in substance as) the unlawful discrimination that was the subject of the terminated complaint; or
-- (b) must arise out of the same (or substantially the same) acts, omissions or practices". (3A): "The application must
-- not be made unless: (a) the court concerned grants leave to make the application; or (b) the complaint was terminated
-- under paragraph 46PH(1)(h); or (c) the complaint was terminated under paragraph 46PH(1B)(b)."
-- s 46POB(1): "A person on whose behalf a representative application is made is not entitled to make a separate
-- application under subsection 46PO(1) in respect of the same subject matter unless the person opts out".
DECLARE `How my complaint ended` IS ONE OF
    `it has not ended: the President is still dealing with it`
    `it was withdrawn with the President's leave (s 46PG)`
    `terminated on request, as a complaint against the Commission (s 46PE)`
    `terminated without inquiry (s 46PF(1)(b))`
    `terminated: issue of public importance (s 46PH(1)(h))`
    `terminated: no reasonable prospect of conciliation (s 46PH(1B)(b))`
    `terminated on another ground in s 46PH`

DECLARE `Who I am in the complaint` IS ONE OF
    `a person on whose behalf it was lodged (including lodging it myself)`
    `a person or trade union that lodged it for others`
    `neither`

DECLARE `May I apply to the court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) without needing the court's leave, given the ground of termination (s 46PO(1), (3A)(b) or (c)); apply within 60 days of the notice (s 46PO(2))`
    `yes: you may apply to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) with the leave the court has granted (s 46PO(1), (3A)(a)); apply within 60 days of the notice (s 46PO(2))`
    `no: on this ground of termination the application must not be made unless the court grants leave (s 46PO(3A), "must not ... unless": the text's no)`
    `no: an application may be made only after the President terminates the complaint and gives notice of it (s 46PO(1), "if": the text's no)`
    `no: a withdrawn complaint is not a terminated complaint, so s 46PO does not open the way to court (s 46PO(1), "if": the text's no)`
    `no: the President has not yet given the written notice of termination (s 46PO(1)(b), "if": the text's no)`
    `no: you are part of a representative application on the same subject matter and have not opted out (s 46POB(1), the text's no)`
    `no: the application must allege the same unlawful discrimination as the complaint, or discrimination arising out of the same acts (s 46PO(3), the text's no)`
    `s 46PO(2A) does not list you: the application is made by an affected person, or by the person or union that lodged the complaint (NOTHING-kind)`

@export May I take my discrimination complaint to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 ss 46PO, 46POB)? Only once the President has terminated it and given written notice; for the same discrimination; and, unless it was ended as a matter of public importance or for no prospect of conciliation, only with the court's leave.
GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How my complaint ended` @desc How your complaint to the Commission ended
      `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President has given the written notice of termination (s 46PH(2))
      `who I am` IS A `Who I am in the complaint` @desc Your place in the complaint
      `same discrimination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the discrimination you would allege in court is the same (or the same in substance) as in the complaint, or arises out of the same (or substantially the same) acts
      `court gave leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has granted leave (permission) to make the application
      `in a representative application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone else has already made a representative application to the court on your behalf about the same subject matter and you have not opted out of it
GIVETH A `May I apply to the court`
`s 46PO -- may I apply to the court` `how it ended` `notice given` `who I am` `same discrimination` `court gave leave` `in a representative application` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `how it ended` EQUALS `it has not ended: the President is still dealing with it`
        THEN `no: an application may be made only after the President terminates the complaint and gives notice of it (s 46PO(1), "if": the text's no)`
        IF `how it ended` EQUALS `it was withdrawn with the President's leave (s 46PG)`
        THEN `no: a withdrawn complaint is not a terminated complaint, so s 46PO does not open the way to court (s 46PO(1), "if": the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `notice given`)
        THEN `no: the President has not yet given the written notice of termination (s 46PO(1)(b), "if": the text's no)`
        IF `who I am` EQUALS `neither`
        THEN `s 46PO(2A) does not list you: the application is made by an affected person, or by the person or union that lodged the complaint (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `in a representative application`
        THEN `no: you are part of a representative application on the same subject matter and have not opted out (s 46POB(1), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `same discrimination`)
        THEN `no: the application must allege the same unlawful discrimination as the complaint, or discrimination arising out of the same acts (s 46PO(3), the text's no)`
        IF     `how it ended` EQUALS `terminated: issue of public importance (s 46PH(1)(h))`
            OR `how it ended` EQUALS `terminated: no reasonable prospect of conciliation (s 46PH(1B)(b))`
        THEN `yes: you may apply to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) without needing the court's leave, given the ground of termination (s 46PO(1), (3A)(b) or (c)); apply within 60 days of the notice (s 46PO(2))`
        IF `court gave leave`
        THEN `yes: you may apply to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) with the leave the court has granted (s 46PO(1), (3A)(a)); apply within 60 days of the notice (s 46PO(2))`
        OTHERWISE `no: on this ground of termination the application must not be made unless the court grants leave (s 46PO(3A), "must not ... unless": the text's no)`

-- s 46PO(2): "The application must be made within 60 days after the date of issue of the notice under subsection
-- 46PH(2), or within such further time as the court concerned allows." The last day is the date of issue plus 60.
@export By when must I apply to the court after my complaint to the Australian Human Rights Commission is terminated (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PO(2))? Within 60 days after the date of issue of the termination notice, unless the court allows further time.
GIVEN `notice issued on` IS A DATE @desc The date of issue of the President's written notice terminating your complaint
GIVETH A DATE
`s 46PO(2) -- the last day to apply to the court` `notice issued on` MEANS `notice issued on` `plus days` 60

DECLARE `Applicant` IS ONE OF `the person applying to the court`
DECLARE `Court step` IS ONE OF `make the application to the court`

-- s 46PO(2) as a duty with a deadline, counted in days from the date of issue of the notice (day 0).
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Applicant` `Court step`
`s 46PO(2) -- apply within 60 days of the termination notice` MEANS
    PARTY `the person applying to the court`
    MUST `make the application to the court`
    WITHIN 60
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person applying to the court` BECAUSE "application not made within 60 days after the date of issue of the termination notice: out of time unless the court allows further time (s 46PO(2))"

GIVEN `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 46PO(2) -- applying on this day is in time` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 46PO(2) -- apply within 60 days of the termination notice` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the person applying to the court` `make the application to the court` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Is my court application in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: made within 60 days after the date of issue of the termination notice (s 46PO(2))`
    `out of time, but within the further time the court has allowed (s 46PO(2))`
    `out of time: it must be made within 60 days after the date of issue of the notice, or such further time as the court allows (s 46PO(2), the text's no)`

@export Is my application to the court about my terminated discrimination complaint in time (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PO(2))? It must be made within 60 days after the date of issue of the termination notice, or within such further time as the court allows.
GIVEN `notice issued on` IS A DATE @desc The date of issue of the President's written notice terminating your complaint
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made (or will make) the application to the court
      `further time allowed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has allowed you further time to apply, covering the day you applied
GIVETH AN `Is my court application in time`
`s 46PO(2) -- is my court application in time` `notice issued on` `applied on` `further time allowed` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 46PO(2) -- applying on this day is in time` `the day applied`
        THEN `in time: made within 60 days after the date of issue of the termination notice (s 46PO(2))`
        IF `further time allowed`
        THEN `out of time, but within the further time the court has allowed (s 46PO(2))`
        OTHERWISE `out of time: it must be made within 60 days after the date of issue of the notice, or such further time as the court allows (s 46PO(2), the text's no)`
    WHERE
        `the raw day` MEANS `the number of days from` `notice issued on` `until` `applied on`
        `the day applied` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

-- s 46PO(4): "If the court concerned is satisfied that there has been unlawful discrimination by any respondent, the
-- court may make such orders (including a declaration of right) as it thinks fit, including any of the following orders
-- or any order to a similar effect: (a) ... declaring that the respondent has committed unlawful discrimination and
-- directing the respondent not to repeat or continue such unlawful discrimination; (b) ... requiring a respondent to
-- perform any reasonable act or course of conduct to redress any loss or damage suffered by an applicant; (c) ...
-- requiring a respondent to employ or re-employ an applicant; (d) ... requiring a respondent to pay to an applicant
-- damages by way of compensation for any loss or damage suffered because of the conduct of the respondent; (e) ...
-- requiring a respondent to vary the termination of a contract or agreement to redress any loss or damage suffered by an
-- applicant; (f) ... declaring that it would be inappropriate for any further action to be taken in the matter."
-- (4A), (5): in a representative application or a Part IVA proceeding, "applicant" includes each person for whom it is
-- made (other than one who opted out) or each group member. No cap on damages is set. An empty list: the "if" fails
-- (the text's no).
DECLARE `Court order` IS ONE OF
    `a declaration that the respondent committed unlawful discrimination, and a direction not to repeat or continue it (s 46PO(4)(a))`
    `an order to perform any reasonable act or course of conduct to redress your loss or damage (s 46PO(4)(b))`
    `an order to employ or re-employ you (s 46PO(4)(c))`
    `an order to pay you damages as compensation for loss or damage caused by the respondent's conduct, with no cap in the Act (s 46PO(4)(d))`
    `an order to vary the termination of a contract or agreement to redress your loss or damage (s 46PO(4)(e))`
    `a declaration that no further action should be taken in the matter (s 46PO(4)(f))`
    `any other order the court thinks fit, including a declaration of right or an order to a similar effect (s 46PO(4))`

@export What can the court order if it finds that the respondent discriminated against me unlawfully (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PO(4))? A declaration and direction not to repeat, redress, employment or re-employment, damages for loss or damage (no cap), varying a terminated contract, or any other order it thinks fit. An empty list means the court has not found unlawful discrimination, so s 46PO(4) gives no order.
GIVEN `court satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that there has been unlawful discrimination by the respondent against you (as applicant, a person in a representative application who has not opted out, or a group member)
GIVETH A LIST OF `Court order`
`s 46PO(4) -- what the court can order` `court satisfied` MEANS
    IF `court satisfied`
    THEN LIST
        `a declaration that the respondent committed unlawful discrimination, and a direction not to repeat or continue it (s 46PO(4)(a))`,
        `an order to perform any reasonable act or course of conduct to redress your loss or damage (s 46PO(4)(b))`,
        `an order to employ or re-employ you (s 46PO(4)(c))`,
        `an order to pay you damages as compensation for loss or damage caused by the respondent's conduct, with no cap in the Act (s 46PO(4)(d))`,
        `an order to vary the termination of a contract or agreement to redress your loss or damage (s 46PO(4)(e))`,
        `a declaration that no further action should be taken in the matter (s 46PO(4)(f))`,
        `any other order the court thinks fit, including a declaration of right or an order to a similar effect (s 46PO(4))`
    ELSE EMPTY

-- s 46PB(4): "A representative complaint may be lodged without the consent of class members." s 46POA(1): "A
-- representative application may not be made without the written consent of each person on whose behalf the
-- application is made (other than the person making the application)." (2)(b): it must include a certificate that each
-- has consented in writing. A Part IVA representative proceeding (Federal Court of Australia Act 1976) is outside this
-- Act's definition of representative application (s 3(1)): NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `Representative step` IS ONE OF
    `a representative complaint to the Commission`
    `a representative application to the court under s 46PO`
    `a representative proceeding in the Federal Court under Part IVA of the Federal Court of Australia Act 1976`

DECLARE `Is my consent needed` IS ONE OF
    `no: a representative complaint may be lodged without the consent of class members (s 46PB(4)); you may withdraw from it by written notice before it is terminated (s 46PC(1))`
    `yes: a representative application may not be made without your written consent (s 46POA(1), the text's no); you may also opt out by the date the court fixes (s 46POB(3))`
    `this Act does not say: a Part IVA proceeding is governed by the Federal Court of Australia Act 1976 (s 3(1) representative application; NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can someone bring a discrimination complaint or court application on my behalf without my consent (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 ss 46PB(4), 46POA(1))? A representative complaint to the Commission needs no consent from the people it is for; a representative application to the court needs each person's written consent.
GIVEN `the step` IS A `Representative step` @desc What is being brought on your behalf
GIVETH AN `Is my consent needed`
`ss 46PB(4), 46POA -- is my consent needed` `the step` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the step`
    WHEN `a representative complaint to the Commission` THEN `no: a representative complaint may be lodged without the consent of class members (s 46PB(4)); you may withdraw from it by written notice before it is terminated (s 46PC(1))`
    WHEN `a representative application to the court under s 46PO` THEN `yes: a representative application may not be made without your written consent (s 46POA(1), the text's no); you may also opt out by the date the court fixes (s 46POB(3))`
    WHEN `a representative proceeding in the Federal Court under Part IVA of the Federal Court of Australia Act 1976` THEN `this Act does not say: a Part IVA proceeding is governed by the Federal Court of Australia Act 1976 (s 3(1) representative application; NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 46POB(2): the court "must fix a date before which a person may opt out". (3): "A person on whose behalf the
-- application is made (other than the person who made the application) may opt out of the proceeding by written notice to
-- the court concerned: (a) before the date so fixed; and (b) in accordance with the rules of court". (4): the date may be
-- extended. (1): having opted out, the person may make a separate application. A period the text fixes: after the date,
-- the text's no. Without written notice: the grant's own limit (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `May I opt out` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may opt out by written notice to the court before the date it fixed (s 46POB(3)); you may then make your own application (s 46POB(1))`
    `no: the person who made the representative application cannot opt out of it (s 46POB(3), "other than": the text's no)`
    `no: opting out must be before the date the court fixed, or extended (s 46POB(3)(a), a period the text fixes: the text's no)`
    `not yet: opting out is by written notice to the court, in accordance with its rules (s 46POB(3)) (NOTHING-kind)`

@export A representative application has been made to the court on my behalf: may I opt out (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46POB)? A person for whom it is made, other than the person who made it, may opt out by written notice to the court before the date the court fixes, and may then bring their own application.
GIVEN `I made it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person who made the representative application
      `before the date` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is still before the opt-out date the court fixed (or extended)
      `written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you give the court a written notice of opting out, in accordance with its rules
GIVETH A `May I opt out`
`s 46POB -- may I opt out` `I made it` `before the date` `written notice` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `I made it`
        THEN `no: the person who made the representative application cannot opt out of it (s 46POB(3), "other than": the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `before the date`)
        THEN `no: opting out must be before the date the court fixed, or extended (s 46POB(3)(a), a period the text fixes: the text's no)`
        IF `written notice`
        THEN `yes: you may opt out by written notice to the court before the date it fixed (s 46POB(3)); you may then make your own application (s 46POB(1))`
        OTHERWISE `not yet: opting out is by written notice to the court, in accordance with its rules (s 46POB(3)) (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `ss 46PP, 46PO(6)-(8) -- Can the court stop things changing while my complaint is dealt with?`

-- s 46PP(1): "At any time after a complaint is lodged with the Commission, the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and
-- Family Court of Australia (Division 2) may grant an interim injunction to maintain: (a) the status quo, as it existed
-- immediately before the complaint was lodged; or (b) the rights of any complainant, respondent or affected person."
-- (2): the application may be made by "the Commission, a complainant, a respondent or an affected person". (3): "The
-- injunction cannot be granted after the complaint has been withdrawn under section 46PG or terminated under section
-- 46PE, paragraph 46PF(1)(b) or section 46PH." (5): no undertaking as to damages may be required.
-- s 46PO(6): once an application is made, the court "may, if it thinks fit, grant an interim injunction pending the
-- determination of the proceedings"; (8): no undertaking as to damages.
-- Before lodging: the period the text fixes ("at any time after") has not begun: the text's no.
DECLARE `Stage of my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `not yet lodged`
    `lodged and still with the President`
    `withdrawn`
    `terminated, with no court application yet`
    `terminated, and I have applied to the court under s 46PO`

DECLARE `Can I ask for an interim injunction` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may ask the court for an interim injunction to keep things as they were before you complained, or to protect your rights, and you cannot be made to give an undertaking as to damages (s 46PP(1), (2), (5))`
    `yes: the court may grant an interim injunction pending the determination of your proceedings, without requiring an undertaking as to damages (s 46PO(6), (8))`
    `no: an interim injunction under s 46PP is available only after a complaint is lodged (s 46PP(1), a period the text fixes: the text's no)`
    `no: an interim injunction under s 46PP cannot be granted after the complaint is withdrawn or terminated (s 46PP(3), the text's no)`

@export Can I ask the court to stop things changing (an interim injunction) while my discrimination complaint or court case is dealt with (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 ss 46PP, 46PO(6))? After lodging a complaint and until it is withdrawn or terminated, you may ask the court to keep the status quo; once you have applied to the court, it may grant one pending the case. You need not give an undertaking as to damages.
GIVEN `the stage` IS A `Stage of my complaint` @desc Where your complaint stands
GIVETH A `Can I ask for an interim injunction`
`ss 46PP, 46PO(6) -- can I ask for an interim injunction` `the stage` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the stage`
    WHEN `not yet lodged` THEN `no: an interim injunction under s 46PP is available only after a complaint is lodged (s 46PP(1), a period the text fixes: the text's no)`
    WHEN `lodged and still with the President` THEN `yes: you may ask the court for an interim injunction to keep things as they were before you complained, or to protect your rights, and you cannot be made to give an undertaking as to damages (s 46PP(1), (2), (5))`
    WHEN `withdrawn` THEN `no: an interim injunction under s 46PP cannot be granted after the complaint is withdrawn or terminated (s 46PP(3), the text's no)`
    WHEN `terminated, with no court application yet` THEN `no: an interim injunction under s 46PP cannot be granted after the complaint is withdrawn or terminated (s 46PP(3), the text's no)`
    WHEN `terminated, and I have applied to the court under s 46PO` THEN `yes: the court may grant an interim injunction pending the determination of your proceedings, without requiring an undertaking as to damages (s 46PO(6), (8))`

§§ `ss 46PQ, 46PT, 46PU -- Representation and help in court`

-- s 46PQ(1): "A party in proceedings under this Division: (a) may appear in person; or (b) may be represented by a
-- barrister or a solicitor; or (c) may be represented by another person who is not a barrister or solicitor, unless the
-- court is of the opinion that it is inappropriate in the circumstances for the other person to appear." (2): "A person,
-- other than a barrister or solicitor, is not entitled to demand or receive any fee or reward, or any payment for
-- expenses, for representing a party".
DECLARE `Who appears for me` IS ONE OF
    `I appear myself`
    `a barrister or solicitor`
    `another person who is not a barrister or solicitor`

DECLARE `May they represent me in court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may appear in person (s 46PQ(1)(a))`
    `yes: you may be represented by a barrister or solicitor (s 46PQ(1)(b))`
    `yes: you may be represented by a person who is not a lawyer, who may not demand or receive any fee, reward or expenses for it (s 46PQ(1)(c), (2))`
    `no: the court is of the opinion that it is inappropriate for that person to appear (s 46PQ(1)(c), "unless": fork F3)`

@export Who may represent me in my discrimination case in the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PQ)? You may appear yourself, use a barrister or solicitor, or be represented by someone else unless the court thinks it inappropriate; a non-lawyer may not be paid for it.
GIVEN `who appears` IS A `Who appears for me` @desc Who would appear for you
      `court finds inappropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a person who is not a lawyer: whether the court is of the opinion that it is inappropriate in the circumstances for that person to appear
GIVETH A `May they represent me in court`
`s 46PQ -- may they represent me in court` `who appears` `court finds inappropriate` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who appears`
    WHEN `I appear myself` THEN `yes: you may appear in person (s 46PQ(1)(a))`
    WHEN `a barrister or solicitor` THEN `yes: you may be represented by a barrister or solicitor (s 46PQ(1)(b))`
    WHEN `another person who is not a barrister or solicitor` THEN
        IF `court finds inappropriate`
        THEN `no: the court is of the opinion that it is inappropriate for that person to appear (s 46PQ(1)(c), "unless": fork F3)`
        ELSE `yes: you may be represented by a person who is not a lawyer, who may not demand or receive any fee, reward or expenses for it (s 46PQ(1)(c), (2))`

-- s 46PT: "The Commission may help a person to prepare the forms required for the person to make an application under
-- this Division." s 46PU(1): a person who "has commenced or proposes to commence proceedings ... under this Division"
-- may apply to the Attorney-General for assistance. (2): "If ... the Attorney-General is satisfied that: (a) it will
-- involve hardship to that person to refuse the application; and (b) in all the circumstances, it is reasonable to grant
-- the application; the Attorney-General may authorise the provision by the Commonwealth to that person ... of such legal
-- or financial assistance in respect of the proceedings as the Attorney-General determines." The "if" fails: the power
-- does not arise (the text's no). Whether it is exercised is the Attorney-General's: "may".
DECLARE `Can I get help with my court case` IS ONE OF
    `yes, it may be given: the Attorney-General may authorise legal or financial assistance from the Commonwealth, on conditions the Attorney-General sets (s 46PU(2)); the Commission may also help you prepare the forms (s 46PT)`
    `no: the Attorney-General may authorise assistance only if satisfied that refusing would cause you hardship and that granting it is reasonable in all the circumstances (s 46PU(2), "if": the text's no); the Commission may still help you prepare the forms (s 46PT)`
    `no: assistance under s 46PU is for a person who has started or proposes to start proceedings under Part IIB Division 2 (s 46PU(1), the text's no)`

@export Can I get legal or financial help from the Commonwealth for my discrimination case in court (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 ss 46PT, 46PU)? You may apply to the Attorney-General, who may grant legal or financial assistance if satisfied that refusing would cause you hardship and that granting is reasonable; the Commission may help you prepare the forms.
GIVEN `started or proposing proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have started, or propose to start, proceedings in the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) under Part IIB
      `hardship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Attorney-General is satisfied that refusing your application would involve hardship to you
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Attorney-General is satisfied that, in all the circumstances, it is reasonable to grant the application
GIVETH A `Can I get help with my court case`
`s 46PU -- can I get help with my court case` `started or proposing proceedings` `hardship` `reasonable` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `started or proposing proceedings`)
        THEN `no: assistance under s 46PU is for a person who has started or proposes to start proceedings under Part IIB Division 2 (s 46PU(1), the text's no)`
        IF `hardship` AND `reasonable`
        THEN `yes, it may be given: the Attorney-General may authorise legal or financial assistance from the Commonwealth, on conditions the Attorney-General sets (s 46PU(2)); the Commission may also help you prepare the forms (s 46PT)`
        OTHERWISE `no: the Attorney-General may authorise assistance only if satisfied that refusing would cause you hardship and that granting it is reasonable in all the circumstances (s 46PU(2), "if": the text's no); the Commission may still help you prepare the forms (s 46PT)`

§§ `s 46PSA -- Costs (the costs protection rule of 2024)`

-- s 46PSA as substituted by the Australian Human Rights Commission Amendment (Costs Protection) Act 2024 (No. 89, 2024;
-- in force 2 October 2024). Its application provision is in the amending Act, which is not deposited: the encoding
-- answers by the compiled text (finding 1).
-- (1): "This section applies to proceedings (including an appeal) in a court that relate to an application made by a
-- person (the applicant) under section 46PO in respect of one or more respondents to a terminated complaint."
-- (2): "Subject to subsection (4), if the applicant is successful in proceedings on one or more grounds, the court must
-- order each respondent against whom the applicant is successful to pay the applicant's costs." (3): "The court may
-- order that the costs to be paid by the respondent be assessed on an indemnity basis or otherwise." (4): "If the court is
-- satisfied that the applicant's unreasonable act or omission caused the applicant to incur costs, the court is not
-- required to order the respondent to pay the costs incurred as a result of that act or omission."
DECLARE `Must the respondent pay my costs` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must order the respondent to pay your costs, and may order them assessed on an indemnity basis (s 46PSA(2), (3))`
    `yes, except: the court must order the respondent to pay your costs, but need not order it to pay the costs your own unreasonable act or omission caused you (s 46PSA(2), (4))`
    `no: s 46PSA(2) requires a costs order only against a respondent against whom you succeed on one or more grounds ("if": the text's no)`
    `s 46PSA does not apply: it covers proceedings, including appeals, relating to an application to the court under s 46PO (s 46PSA(1), a scope limit: NOTHING-kind)`

@export If I win my discrimination case in court, must the respondent pay my legal costs (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PSA(1)-(4), the 2024 costs protection rule)? If you succeed on one or more grounds, the court must order each respondent you succeed against to pay your costs (possibly on an indemnity basis), except costs your own unreasonable conduct caused.
GIVEN `a s 46PO case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings (including an appeal) relate to an application you made to the court under s 46PO after your complaint was terminated
      `successful against this respondent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are successful against this respondent on one or more grounds
      `my unreasonable conduct caused costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that an unreasonable act or omission of yours caused you to incur some of your costs
GIVETH A `Must the respondent pay my costs`
`s 46PSA -- must the respondent pay my costs` `a s 46PO case` `successful against this respondent` `my unreasonable conduct caused costs` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `a s 46PO case`)
        THEN `s 46PSA does not apply: it covers proceedings, including appeals, relating to an application to the court under s 46PO (s 46PSA(1), a scope limit: NOTHING-kind)`
        IF (NOT `successful against this respondent`)
        THEN `no: s 46PSA(2) requires a costs order only against a respondent against whom you succeed on one or more grounds ("if": the text's no)`
        IF `my unreasonable conduct caused costs`
        THEN `yes, except: the court must order the respondent to pay your costs, but need not order it to pay the costs your own unreasonable act or omission caused you (s 46PSA(2), (4))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the court must order the respondent to pay your costs, and may order them assessed on an indemnity basis (s 46PSA(2), (3))`

-- (5): "Subject to subsection (6), the applicant must not be ordered by the court to pay costs incurred by another party
-- to the proceedings." (6): "The applicant may be ordered to pay the costs if: (a) the court is satisfied that the
-- applicant instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause; or (b) the court is satisfied that the
-- applicant's unreasonable act or omission caused the other party to incur the costs; or (c) all of the following apply:
-- (i) the other party is a respondent who was successful in the proceedings; (ii) the respondent does not have a
-- significant power advantage over the applicant; (iii) the respondent does not have significant financial or other
-- resources relative to the applicant." (7): "In the case of a representative application, subsection (6) does not
-- authorise the court concerned to award costs against a person on whose behalf the application is made other than the
-- person who made the application."
-- The answer under (6) is "may": the court's discretion, never decided. Outside (6) the answer is (5)'s flat "must not".
DECLARE `My place in the court case` IS ONE OF
    `I made the application (alone, or as the person who made a representative application)`
    `a representative application was made on my behalf by someone else`

DECLARE `Can I be made to pay their costs` IS ONE OF
    `no: you must not be ordered to pay another party's costs (s 46PSA(5), the text's no)`
    `no: in a representative application, costs cannot be awarded against a person on whose behalf it was made, other than the person who made it (s 46PSA(7), with (5): the text's no)`
    `you may be ordered to pay: the court is satisfied you brought the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause (s 46PSA(6)(a))`
    `you may be ordered to pay the costs your unreasonable act or omission caused the other party (s 46PSA(6)(b))`
    `you may be ordered to pay: the respondent succeeded and has neither a significant power advantage over you nor significantly greater financial or other resources (s 46PSA(6)(c))`
    `s 46PSA does not apply, so this Act does not say: it covers proceedings, including appeals, relating to an application to the court under s 46PO (s 46PSA(1), a scope limit: NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I be ordered to pay the other side's legal costs in my discrimination case in court (Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986 s 46PSA(5)-(7), the 2024 costs protection rule)? Generally no; but you may be if you brought the case vexatiously or without reasonable cause, for costs your unreasonable conduct caused, or if a successful respondent has no significant power or resources advantage over you. A person in a representative application who did not make it cannot be.
GIVEN `a s 46PO case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings (including an appeal) relate to an application to the court under s 46PO after a complaint was terminated
      `my place` IS A `My place in the court case` @desc Whether you made the application, or it was made on your behalf as a representative application
      `vexatious or without reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that the applicant brought the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause
      `my unreasonable conduct caused their costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that an unreasonable act or omission of the applicant caused the other party to incur the costs
      `respondent succeeded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other party is a respondent who was successful in the proceedings
      `respondent has power advantage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that respondent has a significant power advantage over the applicant (for example an employer over an employee)
      `respondent has more resources` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that respondent has significant financial or other resources relative to the applicant
GIVETH A `Can I be made to pay their costs`
`s 46PSA -- can I be made to pay their costs` `a s 46PO case` `my place` `vexatious or without reasonable cause` `my unreasonable conduct caused their costs` `respondent succeeded` `respondent has power advantage` `respondent has more resources` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `a s 46PO case`)
        THEN `s 46PSA does not apply, so this Act does not say: it covers proceedings, including appeals, relating to an application to the court under s 46PO (s 46PSA(1), a scope limit: NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `my place` EQUALS `a representative application was made on my behalf by someone else`
        THEN `no: in a representative application, costs cannot be awarded against a person on whose behalf it was made, other than the person who made it (s 46PSA(7), with (5): the text's no)`
        IF `vexatious or without reasonable cause`
        THEN `you may be ordered to pay: the court is satisfied you brought the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause (s 46PSA(6)(a))`
        IF `my unreasonable conduct caused their costs`
        THEN `you may be ordered to pay the costs your unreasonable act or omission caused the other party (s 46PSA(6)(b))`
        IF `respondent succeeded` AND (NOT `respondent has power advantage`) AND (NOT `respondent has more resources`)
        THEN `you may be ordered to pay: the respondent succeeded and has neither a significant power advantage over you nor significantly greater financial or other resources (s 46PSA(6)(c))`
        OTHERWISE `no: you must not be ordered to pay another party's costs (s 46PSA(5), the text's no)`
